#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Regenerate the clips and stills on the engine's examples page.
#
# Sibling of ./refresh-screenshots, which does the same job for the games. The
# two are kept apart because the games are each their own repo and stage their
# own shot through a --screenshot argument, while the examples are a dozen
# binaries in one repo that take no arguments at all. What stages them is this
# file: a line per example saying how long to let it settle, how long to film,
# and which keys to press while the camera is running.
#
# That is the whole reason it exists. The examples page had stills from the
# 28th of September on it on the 15th of October, and nothing anywhere could
# take them again: they had been shot by hand, once each.
#
# **A still of a physics engine is the wrong picture.** Every example here
# does something, and a dozen of them were being shown off by a photograph of
# the moment after they had finished doing it. So each one is a short silent
# clip that loops, with a still pulled out of the middle of it to stand in
# until the clip loads and to be the picture a readme can use.
#
# macOS only on both counts: it photographs a real window, because nothing in
# the engine renders to a file, and it films the whole screen and crops the
# window out of it, because nothing here films one window. The windows are on
# screen while this runs and the keys go to whatever is frontmost, so leave
# the machine alone while it works.
set -euo pipefail
cd "$(dirname "$0")"

WIDE=1200
# How good a clip is, as x264 counts it. 22 is a quiet number for flat shaded
# geometry on black and puts the whole page under two megabytes.
QUALITY=${QUALITY:-22}
RATE=${RATE:-30}
# How much of the front of each recording is thrown away.
#
# The camera has to be running before the thing it is filming starts, so the
# first seconds of every recording are whatever was on the screen behind it.
# Two is the camera's own second to open plus a run's own to put a window up,
# with a little to spare.
PREROLL=${PREROLL:-2}

# What to film, as: example, wait seconds, film seconds, poster seconds,
# keys.
#
# tower is not here, and that is a decision rather than an oversight. It went
# on 2026-10-16 because cairn is the better example of the same thing: four
# blocks to a level against two, grained wood against one flat colour, and a
# tower that comes apart because taking it apart is the game. It came off the
# page, and then out of the engine altogether. See blitzkit spec 0054.
#
# The wait is how long into the clip the keys start, not how long before the
# camera opens: the camera is running before the example is, so the opening
# is in every clip.
#
# The keys are what somebody would press while watching, sent once the camera
# is running. A key is a letter, `space`, `enter`, or an arrow as
# `left`/`right`/`up`/`down`; `hold:<key>:<seconds>` holds one down, and the
# key may be several joined by a plus, as `hold:w+left:0.8`, which holds them
# together and is how a corner is walked rather than pivoted on; `pin:` is the
# same thing for a walk meant to end against a wall, which the check lets
# through rather than calling stopped;
# `tap:<key>:<many>:<gap>` hits it over and over;
# `shove:<across>:<down>:<held>` presses the mouse at that fraction of the
# window for that long; `zoom:<ticks>` turns the wheel over the middle of it;
# and a number on its own is a wait in seconds.
#
# **The arcade's line walks four rooms.** It used to walk into the hall and
# turn to look at the cabinets, which was right when the hall was all there
# was. Then it walked to the hotel. Then up to the imaginarium. Jake's call,
# 2026-10-29: the arcade, the garden, the pool room and the solarium.
#
# The order is the building's and not a preference. The garden's way in and
# the hole into the solarium are both within two metres of where you start, so
# they come first and cost one walk between them. The hall runs the length of
# the building and is walked once, end to end. The baths are at the far end of
# it, down through the nook's bookcase, so they are last and the clip finishes
# standing in the pool.
#
# **Two doors open on camera, and both of them are the point of their room.**
# The bookcase is spec 0007's secret: pull the book, four hundred of them
# swing out of the way, and the stair down is behind it. The baths' leaf is
# spec 0008's. Spec 0042's checker could not replay either until this route
# needed them, and said so in as many words.
#
# **It never stops to turn at a corner.** Every corner is a `hold:w+left` or a
# `hold:w+right`, walking and turning at once, which draws an arc. Jake watched
# a route that pivoted and said it felt robotic, which it was. A quarter turn
# at a walk sweeps about 1.3 metres across and 2.1 along, so a corner starts
# two metres before its opening rather than at it.
#
# **It does stand still in the two dead ends, on purpose and not for the
# look.** The garden and the solarium are rooms you go into and come back out
# of, and the first version curved through both of them. The solarium's curve
# is what killed the first take: a loop across a vast dark room, a turn in the
# middle of it and a hole a metre or so wide to thread on the way back is dead
# reckoning with nothing to steer by, and the clip went in at twenty seconds
# and never came out. Thirty seconds of black.
#
# Walk in straight, stand, turn on the spot to look, and walk straight back out
# and the error cancels: the way out is the way in and the standing turns move
# you nowhere. The same goes for the garden. A person stopping to look at a
# pond is also what a person does, so nothing is lost but a curve nobody would
# have noticed.
#
# **Both tight targets square up on a wall rather than aiming at them.** The
# bookcase and the door to the baths were both approached by dead reckoning
# first. Both worked in the checker and neither worked twice running on the
# machine: twenty metres of hall put the sight one bookcase along from the one
# that opens, and a couple of degrees of heading is the whole of the baths'
# doorway. A `pin:` walks into the wall on purpose, which fixes the coordinate
# the error was in, and a short strafe off it lands where it should. Use the
# building as the datum wherever a target is tight.
#
# **The climb and the descent are slower than the ground, by about half.**
# Spec 0004's budget empties as you go. That is why the walk down into the
# cellar is held for two seconds rather than the one the distance asks for.
#
# **The aim at the bookcase used to be a coin flip.** Its handle was the one
# book, 8.6 centimetres across, and driving a route at it the window was a
# single seven centimetre step: on it the sight found the book, a step either
# side it found nothing, and the clip either opened the wall or walked into
# it. Spec 0007 widened the handle to the case it stands in on 2026-10-29 and
# the window is a metre now. Do not narrow it again without reading that spec.
#
# **Fifty seconds, and not because the walk takes that long.** It is thirty
# five seconds of holds. The difference is in two places and neither is
# guessable from the table, so it is written down here:
#
#   3.6s before the first key, which is the camera opening, the window
#   lookup, the call to bring it to the front, and the settle above.
#
#   0.4s after every key, which is `play`'s own margin and the process it
#   spawns. Twenty eight keys of that is eleven. A bare wait is a plain sleep
#   and costs nothing on top of itself.
#
# Twenty was tried on a much older route and the walk ran off the end: it
# reached the corridor and never got to the room it was staged for. That clip
# was filmed and looked at before anybody noticed, which is what the line at
# the bottom of this file is for.
#
# The route was not guessed. It was driven against the game's own `--walk`,
# which replays it with no window and refuses one whose turns do not happen or
# whose walks go nowhere. Three things it found are worth knowing before
# anybody edits these numbers.
#
# The turns have to be square: the arrows turn at 2.4 radians a second, so a
# quarter turn is 0.655 seconds and a half turn is 1.31, and getting it wrong
# walks a room at forty degrees to its own axis, scraping a wall for nine
# seconds. This used to read 1.6 radians and call 1.32 a quarter turn, which
# is the tilt rate and a half turn. Spec 0044 rounded the corners of a turn
# and did not change this: the ramps are symmetric, so a hold still sweeps
# what it always swept, held alone or held with a walk.
#
# A hold shorter than the arcade's `AIM_LAGS`, which is 0.2 seconds, sweeps
# less than it asks for and the checker calls it swallowed. That is not a
# fault in the route and it is not a fault in the check: a winding that never
# reaches full speed has no flat middle to make the loss back on. Write turns
# and tilts at a quarter of a second or more.
#
# The way into the nook is one gap at the south end of the hall, between the
# last cabinet and the nearest plinth. Turn west anywhere from z -6.5 to -7.4
# and you go through it; a stride either side of that and you walk into a
# cabinet.

# `enter` is in the list because of the arcade, where it is one of the two
# ways to work the toy you are looking at and the only one that can be filmed.
# That building holds the cursor, so the mouse turns the view rather than
# pointing at anything, and a `shove` would land as a shove of the camera. The
# arcade's route does not press it any more, having stopped going to the desk,
# and it stays in the grammar because the next route to go anywhere with a
# handle on it will want it.
#
# Digits are not in that list and cannot be. `1` and `2` pick cork or granite
# in ripple and neither reaches the window, sent as `keystroke "2"` or as its
# key code: it stays on cork through either. Letters, space and the arrows all
# arrive, so it is the digits and not the mechanism, and ripple is filmed
# dropping cork.
#
# Holding is its own verb because it has to be. rolling drives its ball with
# the arrows held down, and a key sent through System Events is pressed and
# released inside one frame, so the ball never moved: eight seconds of a ball
# sitting still, in an example called rolling. A held key is a keyDown, a
# wait and a keyUp posted through Quartz, which is what `hold` does.
#
# And tapping is a third thing, which took chain to find. Holding a key gives
# one keyDown: a synthetic one does not set the autorepeat going, because
# autorepeat happens below where these events are posted. rolling cannot tell,
# since it reads the key as a state it holds. chain adds 4.2 to the ball's
# speed per event, so a hold is one shove however long it is held, and the
# clip was a wrecking ball arriving at a wall with nothing behind it and
# tipping one brick off the top: 38 of 39 left standing, which Jake read off
# the video as blocks being nudged. Twenty taps a thirtieth of a second apart
# leave 11 of 39 standing and stretch the chain 3.0 per cent against 1.5.
CLIPS=$(cat <<'LIST'
rolling	2.5	9	5	hold:right:1.4 hold:up:1.2 hold:left:1.4 hold:down:1.2 o
stacking	4	13	7	shove:0.66:0.58:1.9 2.6 shove:0.26:0.43:1.9 2.6 k
cubes	0.5	10	6	1 l 2.5 l 2.5 l
teapot	1	7	4	hold:right:2.5 hold:q:1.5
teapot-glass	1	7	5	t 0.4 hold:right:2.5 hold:e:1.5
klein	1	7	4	hold:right:3 hold:q:1.5
klein-glass	1	7	5	m 0.3 t 0.4 hold:right:3
tunnel	0.5	7	4
sierpinski	1	7	5	1.5 up 2 up 2 down
menger	1	7	5	1.5 up 2.5 down
hilbert	1	7	5	1.5 up 2.5 down
ripple	1	9	5.5	1 space 1.6 space 1.6 space 1.6 space
chain	1.5	8	7.4	1 tap:right:20:0.035 3
knot	1	8	4	hold:right:1.6 m 1.2 m 1.0 down 1.2 up
load	3	15	6	1.5 m 1.5 up 1.5 up 1.5 up 2
arcade	2.5	48	23	hold:left:0.655 hold:w:2.4 hold:left:0.5 0.8 hold:right:1.81 hold:w:2.11 hold:right:0.655 hold:w:2.2 0.8 hold:left:0.6 0.8 hold:left:0.71 hold:w:5.2 hold:w+left:0.655 hold:w:1.6 pin:d:0.6 hold:a:0.56 enter 1.4 hold:w:2.0 pin:w:0.9 hold:left:0.655 pin:w:1.4 hold:d:0.27 enter 1.3 hold:w:2.2 hold:right:0.5 1.5
LIST
)

say() { printf '%s\n' "$*"; }

# **Compiled once, not once a press.** Every helper below wrote a Swift file
# and ran it with `swift`, which compiles before it runs. That is a third of
# a second of nothing between one key and the next, and the arcade's staging
# is nineteen keys long: the walk came out seven seconds longer than it was
# written to be, ran off the end of a twenty second clip, and never reached
# the room it was staged for. The timings in the table are what the game
# sees now rather than what it saw plus an unknown amount of compiler.
#
# The source is only written when it has changed, because `cat` touches the
# mtime and a file that is always newer than its binary is always rebuilt.
put_swift() {
  local src=/tmp/blitzkit-$1.swift
  local new

  new=$(cat)

  if [ ! -f "$src" ] || [ "$(cat "$src")" != "$new" ]; then
    printf '%s\n' "$new" > "$src"
    rm -f "/tmp/blitzkit-$1"
  fi
}

swift_run() {
  local name=$1
  shift
  local src=/tmp/blitzkit-$name.swift
  local bin=/tmp/blitzkit-$name

  [ -x "$bin" ] || swiftc -O "$src" -o "$bin" 2>/dev/null || true

  if [ -x "$bin" ]; then
    "$bin" "$@"
  else
    swift "$src" "$@"
  fi
}

# End a run, and mean it. The same two steps as ./refresh-screenshots: a
# window that ignores a polite ask leaves `wait` blocking for ever.
stop() {
  kill "$1" 2>/dev/null || true

  local waited=0
  while kill -0 "$1" 2>/dev/null && [ "$waited" -lt 20 ]; do
    sleep 0.1
    waited=$((waited + 1))
  done

  kill -9 "$1" 2>/dev/null || true
  wait "$1" 2>/dev/null || true
}

# Which window, and where it is in points, via CoreGraphics. There is no
# shell for this.
#
# The examples call their windows "blitzkit: <name>", where the games call
# theirs after the game, which is why this does not take
# ./refresh-screenshots' function as it stands.
window_box() {
  put_swift example-window <<'SWIFT'
import CoreGraphics
import Foundation
let want = (CommandLine.arguments.dropFirst().first ?? "").lowercased()
let on = CGWindowListCopyWindowInfo([.optionOnScreenOnly], kCGNullWindowID) as? [[String: Any]] ?? []
for w in on {
    let name = w[kCGWindowName as String] as? String ?? ""
    if name.lowercased() == want, let b = w[kCGWindowBounds as String] as? [String: Any] {
        let id = w[kCGWindowNumber as String] as? Int ?? 0
        let x = b["X"] as? Double ?? 0, y = b["Y"] as? Double ?? 0
        let wd = b["Width"] as? Double ?? 0, ht = b["Height"] as? Double ?? 0
        print("\(id) \(Int(x)) \(Int(y)) \(Int(wd)) \(Int(ht))")
        break
    }
}
SWIFT
  swift_run example-window "$1"
}

# How many pixels there are to a point on the screen being filmed.
#
# Worked out rather than assumed, because a window's box comes back in points
# and the film comes back in pixels. Taking one for the other crops a quarter
# of the window on a retina screen.
backing() {
  local pixels points
  pixels=$(system_profiler SPDisplaysDataType 2>/dev/null |
    sed -n 's/.*Resolution: \([0-9]*\) x \([0-9]*\).*/\1/p' | head -1)
  points=$(osascript -e 'tell application "Finder" to get bounds of window of desktop' |
    awk -F', *' '{print $3}')

  if [ -n "$pixels" ] && [ -n "$points" ] && [ "$points" -gt 0 ]; then
    echo $((pixels / points))
  else
    echo 2
  fi
}

front() {
  osascript -e "tell application \"System Events\" to tell (first process whose unix id is $1) to set frontmost to true" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
}

# What macOS calls each key this knows about. The numbers are where the keys
# sit on the board and there is no arithmetic in them.
key_code() {
  case "$1" in
    space) echo 49 ;;
    enter) echo 36 ;;
    left) echo 123 ;;
    right) echo 124 ;;
    down) echo 125 ;;
    up) echo 126 ;;
    a) echo 0 ;; s) echo 1 ;; d) echo 2 ;; f) echo 3 ;; h) echo 4 ;;
    g) echo 5 ;; z) echo 6 ;; x) echo 7 ;; c) echo 8 ;; v) echo 9 ;;
    b) echo 11 ;; q) echo 12 ;; w) echo 13 ;; e) echo 14 ;; r) echo 15 ;;
    y) echo 16 ;; t) echo 17 ;; o) echo 31 ;; u) echo 32 ;; i) echo 34 ;;
    p) echo 35 ;; l) echo 37 ;; j) echo 38 ;; k) echo 40 ;; n) echo 45 ;;
    m) echo 46 ;;
    *) echo "" ;;
  esac
}

# Hold a key down, properly, through Quartz.
#
# System Events has no way to say it: its `key code` is a press and a release
# with nothing in between, which is right for a key that does a thing and
# wrong for a key that means keep doing this. See the note on the table.
# Hit a key over and over, as fast as a hand never could.
#
# Not the same as holding it: see the note on the table. A key that means do
# this now wants this, and a key that means keep doing this wants `hold`.
tap() {
  local code
  code=$(key_code "$1")
  [ -n "$code" ] || { say "   no key code for $1"; return; }

  put_swift tap <<'SWIFT'
import CoreGraphics
import Foundation
let a = CommandLine.arguments.dropFirst()
guard let code = UInt16(a.first ?? ""), let many = Int(a.dropFirst().first ?? ""),
      let gap = Double(a.dropFirst(2).first ?? "") else { exit(1) }
let src = CGEventSource(stateID: .hidSystemState)
for _ in 0..<many {
    CGEvent(keyboardEventSource: src, virtualKey: CGKeyCode(code), keyDown: true)?.post(tap: .cghidEventTap)
    Thread.sleep(forTimeInterval: 0.004)
    CGEvent(keyboardEventSource: src, virtualKey: CGKeyCode(code), keyDown: false)?.post(tap: .cghidEventTap)
    Thread.sleep(forTimeInterval: gap)
}
SWIFT
  swift_run tap "$code" "$2" "$3" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
}

# Click the window, at a fraction of the way across and down it.
#
# Through Quartz, like the keys, and for the same reason: System Events'
# `click at` reaches these windows as nothing. tower is forty blocks you
# knock a brick out of to watch fall, and it was filmed standing there for
# nine seconds, which Jake read off the page as demonstrating nothing.
#
# A fraction and not a point, because the window is wherever it opened. The
# third number is how long the button is held, which is a click left out and
# a wind up in stacking: hold on a sphere to wind it, let go to shove it.
shove() {
  local across=$1 down=$2 held=${3:-0.06}
  local at_x at_y
  at_x=$(awk -v x="$WIN_X" -v w="$WIN_W" -v f="$across" 'BEGIN{printf "%d", x + w * f}')
  at_y=$(awk -v y="$WIN_Y" -v h="$WIN_H" -v f="$down" 'BEGIN{printf "%d", y + h * f}')

  put_swift shove <<'SWIFT'
import CoreGraphics
import Foundation
let a = CommandLine.arguments.dropFirst()
guard let x = Double(a.first ?? ""), let y = Double(a.dropFirst().first ?? ""),
      let held = Double(a.dropFirst(2).first ?? "") else { exit(1) }
let at = CGPoint(x: x, y: y)
let src = CGEventSource(stateID: .hidSystemState)
CGEvent(mouseEventSource: src, mouseType: .mouseMoved, mouseCursorPosition: at, mouseButton: .left)?.post(tap: .cghidEventTap)
Thread.sleep(forTimeInterval: 0.08)
CGEvent(mouseEventSource: src, mouseType: .leftMouseDown, mouseCursorPosition: at, mouseButton: .left)?.post(tap: .cghidEventTap)
Thread.sleep(forTimeInterval: held)
CGEvent(mouseEventSource: src, mouseType: .leftMouseUp, mouseCursorPosition: at, mouseButton: .left)?.post(tap: .cghidEventTap)
SWIFT
  swift_run shove "$at_x" "$at_y" "$held" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
}

# Put the pointer out of the frame before the camera opens.
#
# The recording below asks for no cursor and this macOS draws one anyway, and
# the pointer stays wherever the last example's shove left it: rolling's still
# came out on 2026-10-28 with an arrow sitting in the middle of its tray. The
# top left corner of the screen is above every window's content, so no crop
# reaches it. An example that shoves or turns the wheel puts the pointer back
# into its own frame, which is the example working and is meant to be seen.
park() {
  put_swift park <<'SWIFT'
import CoreGraphics
let src = CGEventSource(stateID: .hidSystemState)
CGEvent(mouseEventSource: src, mouseType: .mouseMoved,
        mouseCursorPosition: CGPoint(x: 2, y: 2), mouseButton: .left)?.post(tap: .cghidEventTap)
SWIFT
  swift_run park >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
}

# Turn the wheel over the middle of the window.
#
# Most of these put the thing they are about in the middle of the frame and a
# long way off, with the rest of the window black. A camera that moves is the
# example's own control and costs nothing to use.
wheel() {
  local ticks=$1
  local at_x at_y
  at_x=$(awk -v x="$WIN_X" -v w="$WIN_W" 'BEGIN{printf "%d", x + w * 0.5}')
  at_y=$(awk -v y="$WIN_Y" -v h="$WIN_H" 'BEGIN{printf "%d", y + h * 0.5}')

  put_swift wheel <<'SWIFT'
import CoreGraphics
import Foundation
let a = CommandLine.arguments.dropFirst()
guard let x = Double(a.first ?? ""), let y = Double(a.dropFirst().first ?? ""),
      let ticks = Int(a.dropFirst(2).first ?? "") else { exit(1) }
let at = CGPoint(x: x, y: y)
let src = CGEventSource(stateID: .hidSystemState)
CGEvent(mouseEventSource: src, mouseType: .mouseMoved, mouseCursorPosition: at, mouseButton: .left)?.post(tap: .cghidEventTap)
Thread.sleep(forTimeInterval: 0.05)
for _ in 0..<abs(ticks) {
    let e = CGEvent(scrollWheelEvent2Source: src, units: .line, wheelCount: 1,
                    wheel1: Int32(ticks > 0 ? 1 : -1), wheel2: 0, wheel3: 0)
    e?.location = at
    e?.post(tap: .cghidEventTap)
    Thread.sleep(forTimeInterval: 0.03)
}
SWIFT
  swift_run wheel "$at_x" "$at_y" "$ticks" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
}

# Holds one key, or several at once joined by a plus, for that long.
#
# **Several at once is what walks a corner.** A route that stops, pivots and
# sets off again reads as a machine however smooth the pivot is, which is what
# Jake said after watching the arcade turn with spec 0044's easing in it. The
# building turns you with the arrows and walks you with w, both off held keys,
# so `hold:w+left:0.8` is a walk that curves. Down in the order given and up
# in the other, which is how a hand comes off two keys.
hold() {
  local codes=() code key
  local old=$IFS
  IFS='+'
  # shellcheck disable=SC2206
  local keys=($1)
  IFS=$old

  for key in "${keys[@]}"; do
    code=$(key_code "$key")
    [ -n "$code" ] || { say "   no key code for $key"; return; }
    codes+=("$code")
  done

  put_swift hold <<'SWIFT'
import CoreGraphics
import Foundation
let args = Array(CommandLine.arguments.dropFirst())
guard let secs = Double(args.last ?? "") else { exit(1) }
let codes = args.dropLast().compactMap { UInt16($0) }
guard !codes.isEmpty else { exit(1) }
let src = CGEventSource(stateID: .hidSystemState)
for code in codes {
    CGEvent(keyboardEventSource: src, virtualKey: CGKeyCode(code), keyDown: true)?.post(tap: .cghidEventTap)
}
Thread.sleep(forTimeInterval: secs)
for code in codes.reversed() {
    CGEvent(keyboardEventSource: src, virtualKey: CGKeyCode(code), keyDown: false)?.post(tap: .cghidEventTap)
}
SWIFT
  swift_run hold "${codes[@]}" "$2" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
}

press() {
  case "$1" in
    space) osascript -e 'tell application "System Events" to key code 49' >/dev/null 2>&1 || true ;;
    # by its code, like the four above. `keystroke "enter"` types the five
    # letters of the word, which in the arcade is five keys that do other
    # things: the first clip filmed with it worked a toy and typed at the
    # building instead of opening the room it was aiming at.
    enter) osascript -e 'tell application "System Events" to key code 36' >/dev/null 2>&1 || true ;;
    left) osascript -e 'tell application "System Events" to key code 123' >/dev/null 2>&1 || true ;;
    right) osascript -e 'tell application "System Events" to key code 124' >/dev/null 2>&1 || true ;;
    down) osascript -e 'tell application "System Events" to key code 125' >/dev/null 2>&1 || true ;;
    up) osascript -e 'tell application "System Events" to key code 126' >/dev/null 2>&1 || true ;;
    *) osascript -e "tell application \"System Events\" to keystroke \"$1\"" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true ;;
  esac
}

# Press a run's keys on its own clock, while the camera is running.
#
# Fronted before every key and not once before the lot. Once was enough on a
# quiet machine and not on a busy one: a run came out with one ball in the
# pool instead of four and its title bar grey in the picture, because the
# window had stopped being frontmost partway and the rest of the keys went
# somewhere else.
#
# **The gap between two keys is what spec 0044's winding off runs in.** The
# arcade eases its arrows on and off over `AIM_LAGS`, which is 0.2 seconds,
# and a hold only sweeps what it asks for if that winding off finishes before
# the next key goes down.
#
# The gap is this sleep, the `front` above it, and the spawning and exiting of
# the process that posts the key: about four tenths a key, measured off a
# clip rather than reasoned about. That is comfortably over 0.2.
#
# It is also a soft edge rather than a sharp one, which is worth knowing
# before anybody tunes either number. Smoothstep reaches nought with no slope,
# so the last of the winding off is where the view is barely moving: cutting
# the final twentieth of it costs about a thousandth of the turn. This gets
# dangerous when the gap is less than half of `AIM_LAGS`, not when it is a
# hair under.
#
# Do not raise this sleep to buy room. It is every example's gap, and two of
# them have a tenth of a second of slack in their film: teapot-glass runs 8.9
# seconds of keys inside 9, and rolling 10.3 inside 11. Raising it means
# widening those films in the same change.
play() {
  local pid=$1
  shift

  for key in "$@"; do
    case "$key" in
      [0-9]*) sleep "$key" ;;
      hold:*|pin:*)
        front "$pid"
        sleep 0.15
        hold "$(echo "$key" | cut -d: -f2)" "$(echo "$key" | cut -d: -f3)"
        ;;
      tap:*)
        front "$pid"
        sleep 0.15
        tap "$(echo "$key" | cut -d: -f2)" "$(echo "$key" | cut -d: -f3)" \
          "$(echo "$key" | cut -d: -f4)"
        ;;
      zoom:*)
        front "$pid"
        sleep 0.15
        wheel "$(echo "$key" | cut -d: -f2)"
        sleep 0.2
        ;;
      shove:*)
        front "$pid"
        sleep 0.15
        shove "$(echo "$key" | cut -d: -f2)" "$(echo "$key" | cut -d: -f3)" \
          "$(echo "$key" | cut -d: -f4)"
        sleep 0.2
        ;;
      *)
        front "$pid"
        sleep 0.15
        press "$key"
        sleep 0.15
        ;;
    esac
  done
}

wanted=("$@")

say "== building the examples"
(cd blitzkit && cargo build --release --examples --quiet)
(cd arcade && cargo build --release --quiet)

# Where cargo put them, asked rather than assumed. The repos here share one
# target directory at the project root, so blitzkit/target does not exist and
# looking for the binaries there finds nothing.
TARGET=$(cd blitzkit && cargo metadata --format-version 1 --no-deps |
  python3 -c 'import json,sys; print(json.load(sys.stdin)["target_directory"])')/release
ARCADE=$TARGET/arcade

# media/ at the engine's root and not site/static/media, which is a copy
# site/scripts/build takes at build time and git ignores. Writing to the copy
# is writing to something the next build overwrites, which is where the first
# version of this put fourteen pictures.
OUT=blitzkit/media
SCALE=$(backing)
say "   $SCALE pixels to a point"

made=0
missed=0

while IFS=$'\t' read -r name settle seconds poster keys; do
  [ -n "$name" ] || continue

  if [ ${#wanted[@]} -gt 0 ]; then
    found=0
    for one in "${wanted[@]}"; do
      [ "$one" = "$name" ] && found=1
    done
    [ "$found" = 1 ] || continue
  fi

  say "== $name"

  # The example to run, which is not always what the file is called: teapot
  # and teapot-glass are the same binary filmed in its two states, which is
  # what the page has always shown and what the readme still shows as two
  # stills.
  runs=${name%-glass}

  # and where it lives. The arcade is a binary in its own repo rather than an
  # example in the engine's, and it is on the site like the examples are, so
  # it is filmed like them.
  if [ "$runs" = arcade ]; then
    binary=$ARCADE
    titled="arcade"
  else
    binary="$TARGET/examples/$runs"
    titled="blitzkit: $runs"
  fi

  # **Asked whether the route still works before anything is filmed.** Spec
  # 0042. The arcade's panel takes all four arrows while the sight is on it,
  # so a route that walks past the panel stops turning and keeps holding keys
  # at a camera that is pointed at a wall. That is what the clip published on
  # the 26th of October was, and no frame of it looks wrong.
  #
  # The game is asked rather than this file knowing the rule, because the rule
  # is the game's and a copy of it here would be a second account of which
  # arrows are yours. The route goes the other way for the same reason: it is
  # written once, in the table above, and handed over.
  #
  # It answers for the walking too: a held w that covers less than half the
  # ground it asked for was stopped by something, which is how the hotel's
  # threshold turned up. It stops you dead on one heading and lets you
  # through on both of the ones either side of it.
  #
  # Only the arcade, which is the only one with a panel and the only one that
  # answers this. The rest open a window at anything they do not recognise.
  if [ "$runs" = arcade ] && ! "$binary" "--walk=$keys"; then
    say "   the staged route does not go where it says, so nothing was filmed"
    missed=$((missed + 1))
    continue
  fi

  # Opened once to find out where its window lands, then closed, because the
  # camera has to be running before the thing it is filming starts.
  #
  # **The opening is the shot.** Filmed the other way round, with the window
  # up and the camera opening a second later, three of these came out as
  # photographs with a soundtrack: rolling settled before the first frame and
  # changed by 85 decibels of nothing across four seconds, and stacking and
  # tower were not much better. What a tower of forty blocks does is fall
  # into place, and that had been happening off camera every time.
  #
  # The window goes back where it was, because winit puts it there: these all
  # ask for the same size and get the same place. Checked rather than hoped,
  # and a run whose window moves says so.
  "$binary" >/dev/null 2>&1 </dev/null &
  finding=$!
  sleep 1.5
  read -r id x y w h <<< "$(window_box "$titled" || true)"
  stop "$finding"

  if [ -z "${id:-}" ]; then
    say "   no window called '$titled' came up"
    missed=$((missed + 1))
    continue
  fi

  WIN_X=$x
  WIN_Y=$y
  WIN_W=$w
  WIN_H=$h

  # -nostdin, and every child gets /dev/null besides. The table this loop is
  # reading comes in on stdin, and ffmpeg reads stdin for keyboard commands:
  # it ate the rest of the list, so the run went from the first example
  # straight to a line that began "7" and died on it.
  # recorded fast and rough, then cut and encoded properly. One pass cannot
  # do it: the front of the recording is the desktop behind the window, and
  # trimming that off a file still being written is not a thing.
  park
  raw=$(mktemp -t "$name").mp4
  ffmpeg -nostdin -hide_banner -loglevel error -f avfoundation -capture_cursor 0 \
    -framerate "$RATE" -i "3" -t "$((seconds + PREROLL))" \
    -vf "crop=$((w * SCALE)):$((h * SCALE)):$((x * SCALE)):$((y * SCALE)),scale=$WIDE:-2" \
    -c:v libx264 -preset ultrafast -crf 16 -pix_fmt yuv420p \
    "$raw" -y >/dev/null 2>&1 </dev/null &
  filming=$!

  # the camera takes about a second to open, and the run starts once it has
  sleep 1.2
  "$binary" >/dev/null 2>&1 </dev/null &
  running=$!
  sleep 0.8

  read -r again rx ry _ _ <<< "$(window_box "$titled" || true)"
  if [ -n "${again:-}" ] && { [ "$rx" != "$x" ] || [ "$ry" != "$y" ]; }; then
    say "   its window came back at $rx $ry rather than $x $y, so the crop is off"
  fi

  front "$running"
  sleep "$settle"
  # shellcheck disable=SC2086
  [ -n "${keys:-}" ] && play "$running" $keys

  # **And fronted once more, for the tail.** `play` fronts the window before
  # every key and then stops, so from the last key to the end of the film
  # nothing is holding it up. Whatever comes forward in that gap is filmed
  # instead, cropped to the window's own rectangle: the arcade's clip ended on
  # two seconds of the chat window it was being written in. The longer the
  # rest at the end of a route, the wider that hole.
  front "$running"

  wait "$filming" 2>/dev/null || true
  stop "$running"

  if [ ! -s "$raw" ]; then
    say "   nothing came out of the camera"
    rm -f "$raw"
    missed=$((missed + 1))
    continue
  fi

  ffmpeg -nostdin -hide_banner -loglevel error -ss "$PREROLL" -i "$raw" \
    -c:v libx264 -preset slow -crf "$QUALITY" -pix_fmt yuv420p -movflags +faststart \
    -an "$OUT/$name.mp4" -y >/dev/null 2>&1 </dev/null
  rm -f "$raw"

  # and a still out of the moment this one is about, which is the poster the
  # clip shows before it loads and the picture a readme can use.
  #
  # Written down per example rather than taken at some fraction of the
  # length. Six tenths of the way through chain is a floor covered in bricks
  # with the ball swung back up out of frame, which is a picture of the
  # aftermath and not of a wrecking ball. Not the first frame either, which
  # for a thing about to happen is a picture of nothing happening.
  #
  # Pick a second the picture holds across a whole swing rather than one that
  # catches an instant. chain's was 3.4, a few tenths before the ball struck,
  # and on 2026-10-28 a take ran the strike late and the still came out as a
  # wall nobody had touched. It is 7.4 now: the ball is down at the bottom of
  # its arc and the wall is in pieces, which stays true however the swing
  # drifts. Where the clip is a thing happening once, the poster wants the
  # state it leaves behind, not the frame it happens in.
  ffmpeg -nostdin -hide_banner -loglevel error -ss "${poster:-1}" \
    -i "$OUT/$name.mp4" -vframes 1 "$OUT/$name.png" -y >/dev/null 2>&1 </dev/null

  # written down as it is filmed, so ./check-fresh can say how far behind this
  # clip is without anybody having to remember. The arcade has a repo of its
  # own on top of the engine and the rest do not, which is the whole of why
  # this takes two commits rather than one.
  if [ "$runs" = arcade ]; then
    ./stamp "$OUT/MADE" --from arcade "$name.mp4" "$name.png"
  else
    ./stamp "$OUT/MADE" "$name.mp4" "$name.png"
  fi

  say "   $OUT/$name.mp4 and $OUT/$name.png, $WIDE wide"
  made=$((made + 1))
done <<< "$CLIPS"

say ""
if [ "$missed" -gt 0 ]; then
  say "$made made, $missed missed."
else
  say "$made made."
fi
say "Watch them before committing. A staged clip can stage the wrong thing,"
say "and the keys go to whatever is frontmost, which is not always the window."
